#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Prepare a PostgreSQL SQL dump for DuckDB at build time.

A thin fork of ../../../sqlite/scripts/pgsql/transform. Both hooks solve the
same problem -- several of our datasets ship only as PostgreSQL dumps -- but
DuckDB's parser *is* PostgreSQL-derived, so almost every rewrite the SQLite
hook performs is unnecessary here and would only lose fidelity. What is left is
the short list of things DuckDB genuinely cannot do, each verified against
DuckDB 1.5.5 (the pinned engine) with the actual dumps behind the eight tags
that use this hook (world, iso3166, frenchtowns, usda, pgexercises, dellstore,
northwind, sportsdb).

Kept, with the error DuckDB raises without it:

  * transcode to UTF-8. world / usda / dellstore ship Latin-1 bytes; DuckDB
    validates UTF-8 in string literals and aborts on the first accented town or
    author name.
  * ``COPY tbl (cols) FROM stdin`` + TEXT-format data + ``\\.`` -> batched
    multi-row ``INSERT``s. DuckDB has ``COPY``, but only from a *file*: there
    is no inline-data form, so the data rows parse as SQL and fail
    ("syntax error at or near ..."). PGSQL_COPY_CSV (below) swaps the
    ``INSERT``s for a real file-based ``COPY``, for dumps big enough to care.
  * drop ``SET ...``. Every spelling in these dumps fails: unknown parameters
    (``client_encoding``, ``default_with_oids``) raise a Catalog Error, and
    ``standard_conforming_strings = on`` / ``SET SESSION CHARACTERISTICS`` /
    the multi-value ``search_path = cd, pg_catalog`` are outright parse errors.
  * drop ``SELECT pg_catalog.setval(...)``: DuckDB has ``nextval``/``currval``
    but no ``setval`` (Catalog Error), and a fresh load never needs it -- the
    dumps' id columns are all written explicitly. Likewise
    ``SELECT pg_catalog.set_config('search_path', '', false)``, the line
    modern pg_dump writes in place of a ``SET search_path`` ("Catalog Error:
    Scalar Function with name set_config does not exist!").
  * drop ``GRANT`` / ``REVOKE`` and psql's ``\\connect`` / ``\\.`` (parse errors),
    and ``CREATE``/``DROP SCHEMA`` together with ``public.`` / ``cd.``
    qualifiers, which flattens every dataset into DuckDB's default ``main``
    schema. (pgexercises is the one dump with a schema: it creates ``cd``,
    then relies on ``search_path`` -- which is dropped above -- for its
    unqualified ``CREATE TABLE``s while its indexes say ``cd.bookings``. Keeping
    the schema would leave tables in one place and indexes pointing at
    another.)
  * strip ``USING btree`` / ``USING hash`` / ``USING lsm`` from ``CREATE INDEX``
    (Binder Error: Unknown index type: BTREE), inline and on its own line --
    pgexercises' indexes wrap onto four lines.
  * strip ``WITH (OIDS ...)`` / ``WITHOUT OIDS`` (Not implemented Error).
  * strip ``::regclass``, the one PostgreSQL cast DuckDB has no type for
    (Catalog Error: Type with name regclass does not exist!). Stripping it
    rather than the whole ``DEFAULT nextval('s'::regclass)`` keeps the default
    working, since DuckDB does have sequences.
  * ``serial`` -> ``integer`` (and ``bigserial``/``smallserial``). DuckDB has no
    ``serial`` pseudo-type (Catalog Error: Type with name serial does not
    exist!); frenchtowns and dellstore declare their id columns with it and
    supply every value in the data, so a plain integer column is faithful.
  * drop table-level ``FOREIGN KEY`` constraints from ``CREATE TABLE``, with
    the comma that precedes them -- the same rewrite the scripts/chinook hook
    makes, for the same reason plus one more. DuckDB rejects referential
    actions outright ("FOREIGN KEY constraints cannot use CASCADE, SET NULL or
    SET DEFAULT", which is pgexercises' ``members.recommendedby``), and even
    with the actions stripped it validates the reference per *row* where
    PostgreSQL validates per statement -- so pgexercises' self-referencing
    multi-row ``INSERT`` (member 1 recommended by a member listed later) fails
    with a Constraint Error. Because the ``ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT ...
    FOREIGN KEY`` form is unsupported anyway (below), dropping these keeps FK
    handling uniform across both spellings.
  * ``ALTER TABLE`` is filtered rather than dropped wholesale (the SQLite hook
    can only drop): DuckDB implements ``ADD CONSTRAINT ... PRIMARY KEY``, so
    those survive and world/usda/dellstore keep their primary keys, while
    ``ADD CONSTRAINT ... FOREIGN KEY`` / ``UNIQUE`` (Not implemented Error: No
    support for that ALTER TABLE option yet!), ``ADD CONSTRAINT ... EXCLUDE``
    (parse error), ``ALTER COLUMN ... ADD GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY`` (Not
    implemented Error) and ``OWNER TO`` (parse error) are dropped.
  * drop stored functions, which DuckDB has no equivalent for -- both the
    classic quoted/dollar-quoted ``AS``-body form (dellstore's
    ``new_customer``, which ends at its ``LANGUAGE plpgsql;`` line) and the
    PostgreSQL 14+ SQL-standard body form (``LANGUAGE sql STABLE`` then
    ``RETURN <expr>;``, which the airlines demo uses three times). The second
    shape has no ``LANGUAGE <x>;`` terminator at all, so a hook that only
    looked for one swallowed the rest of the file; the ``AS``-body detection
    that gates the ``RETURN`` terminator is what keeps a ``RETURN`` *inside* a
    quoted PL/pgSQL body from ending the block early.
  * drop ``CREATE DOMAIN`` blocks, to the terminating ``;`` (parse error).
    sportsdb declares ``primary_id AS integer`` and never uses it, so nothing
    downstream refers to the name.

Deleted from the SQLite hook, because DuckDB does not need them:

  * ``::type`` cast stripping. DuckDB speaks PostgreSQL casts; world's
    ``continent = 'Asia'::text`` CHECK constraint loads verbatim.
  * lower-casing table identifiers. DuckDB identifiers are case-insensitive
    (and case-preserving), so a dump that defines ``Regions`` and loads
    ``regions`` resolves either way -- frenchtowns does exactly that.
  * ``DEFAULT nextval(...)`` stripping and the ``CREATE``/``ALTER SEQUENCE``
    block drop: DuckDB has real sequences. (No dump here declares one
    standalone; ``serial`` is handled above.)
  * ``COMMENT ON`` dropping: DuckDB supports ``COMMENT ON TABLE`` and
    ``COMMENT ON COLUMN``.
  * the ``ALTER TABLE ONLY`` -> ``ALTER TABLE`` rewrite: DuckDB accepts ``ONLY``.
  * the ``PRAGMA synchronous=OFF`` / ``journal_mode`` preamble, which is
    SQLite-only and would itself be a Catalog Error here.

A dataset whose dump needs more than the shared rules opts in via environment
knobs, set by a thin dataset-specific transform hook that then delegates here
(see duckdb/scripts/northwind and duckdb/scripts/airlines). Every one defaults
off, so the symlinked datasets' output is byte-identical with or without them:

  PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA=1   rewrite the empty bytea literal ``'\\x'`` to ``''``
                        (DuckDB: "Invalid hex escape code"). Opt-in because
                        the hook has no column types, and in a *text* column
                        the same token is the literal two-character string
                        ``\\x`` -- see EMPTY_BYTEA_RE below.
  PGSQL_EXTRA_SCHEMAS   extra schema qualifiers to strip besides public/cd
                        (space- or comma-separated), e.g. ``bookings`` for the
                        airlines demo, whose every object is qualified to it.
                        COPY statements already de-qualify any schema; this
                        extends the DDL/INSERT/COMMENT rewriting. Mirrors the
                        SQLite hook's knob of the same name.
  PGSQL_DROP_VIEWS=1    drop ``CREATE [OR REPLACE] [MATERIALIZED] VIEW``
                        statements (buffered to their ``;``) *and* the
                        ``COMMENT ON VIEW`` / ``COMMENT ON COLUMN <view>.<col>``
                        statements that name one of them. The SQLite twin only
                        has to drop the views, because it drops every
                        ``COMMENT ON`` anyway; here a comment left pointing at
                        a view that no longer exists is a Catalog Error
                        ("View with name ... does not exist!"). The smoke test
                        counts base tables only, so a dropped view costs no
                        rows.
  PGSQL_TYPE_MAP        column-type substitutions applied inside ``CREATE
                        TABLE`` statements, as space-separated ``from=to``
                        pairs, e.g.
                        ``"jsonb=json point=text tstzrange=text integer[]=text"``.
                        A trailing ``[]`` in the key matches a PostgreSQL array
                        declaration with any spacing. Scoped to ``CREATE
                        TABLE`` (which is buffered whole anyway) so a value or
                        an identifier elsewhere is never touched, and opt-in
                        because only the dataset knows that a type DuckDB
                        lacks is better carried as text than dropped.
  PGSQL_COPY_CSV=1      write each ``COPY ... FROM stdin`` block out as a
                        sibling ``<table>.csv`` and emit DuckDB's file-based
                        ``COPY <table> (<cols>) FROM '<table>.csv' (FORMAT CSV,
                        ...)`` in place of the batched ``INSERT``s. Same rows,
                        but through DuckDB's vectorized CSV reader instead of
                        its SQL parser -- which is what makes the airlines
                        demo's 10.7M rows loadable in minutes rather than
                        hours. Opt-in because it leaves files behind next to
                        the SQL (harmless: only /data is copied into the final
                        image, and the six small dumps have no reason to pay
                        for it). See csv_field() for how NULL is kept distinct
                        from the empty string.

Every other dataset symlinks its scripts/<dataset> directory to scripts/pgsql,
exactly like the SQLite side; the conversion is dataset-agnostic and keyed only
on $SQL_FILES (the files the loader stage cats into the duckdb CLI), which it
rewrites in place.

Memory: the file is streamed a line at a time and the result written to a
sibling temporary that replaces it atomically, rather than read whole and
split. The airlines dump is 499 MB / 10.7M lines, which as a Python string plus
a list of lines peaks over 2 GB -- a third of the build VM -- for no gain,
since every rule here is either per-line or per-statement and statements are
short.
"""
import codecs
import glob
import os
import re
import sys

BATCH = 500

# Bytes read per chunk when sniffing a file's encoding (see detect_encoding).
CHUNK = 1 << 20

# An empty PostgreSQL bytea is written `'\x'` -- the hex format's `\x` prefix
# with no digits after it. DuckDB's BLOB parser rejects it ("Conversion Error:
# Invalid hex escape code encountered in string -> blob conversion"), while a
# non-empty `'\x89AB'` converts fine; `''` is the same empty value spelled in a
# way both engines accept. northwind carries 17 of them (categories.picture,
# employees.photo).
#
# Opt-in via PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA=1, and applied to the file as text, because the
# hook has no column types and so cannot tell a bytea column from a text one:
# in a *text* column the identical source token is the literal two-character
# string `\x`, which an always-on rewrite would silently empty. The pattern is
# the entire literal -- quote, backslash, x, quote -- so `'\x89AB'`, a value
# that merely ends in `\x` (`'C:\x'`), and two adjacent literals that happen to
# abut (`'a\', 'x'`) are all untouched. The one false positive left is a text
# value that embeds `'\x'` between doubled quotes, which is exactly why this is
# per-dataset rather than unconditional.
EMPTY_BYTEA_RE = re.compile(r"'\\x'")

# A COPY header: COPY <table> (<cols>) FROM stdin [DELIMITER 'x'];
COPY_RE = re.compile(
    r"^COPY\s+([^\s(]+)\s*\(([^)]*)\)\s+FROM\s+stdin"
    r"(?:\s+DELIMITER\s+'([^']*)')?\s*;",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# Whole lines that DuckDB cannot parse or execute and that a fresh load does
# not need. See the module docstring for the error each one avoids.
DROP_LINE_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(SET\s|SELECT\s+pg_catalog\.(setval|set_config)|GRANT\s|REVOKE\s|"
    r"WITHOUT\s+OIDS|USING\s+(btree|hash|lsm)\b|\\connect|\\c\s|\\\."
    r"|CREATE\s+SCHEMA|DROP\s+SCHEMA|CREATE\s+DATABASE|DROP\s+DATABASE)\b",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# CREATE DOMAIN has no DuckDB equivalent (parse error) and is dropped whole,
# to its terminating ';' -- the same buffered-block drop DROP_BLOCK_RE performs
# in the SQLite hook. sportsdb declares `CREATE DOMAIN primary_id AS integer;`
# and then never uses it (every column that would carry it is spelled
# `integer`/`serial` outright), so nothing downstream refers to the name.
DROP_BLOCK_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*CREATE\s+DOMAIN\b", re.IGNORECASE)

# ALTER TABLE is buffered to its terminating ';' and then filtered, because
# DuckDB implements exactly one of the forms these dumps use.
ALTER_TABLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*ALTER\s+TABLE\b", re.IGNORECASE)
ALTER_KEEP_RE = re.compile(r"\bADD\s+CONSTRAINT\b.*\bPRIMARY\s+KEY\b",
                           re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL)
ALTER_REJECT_RE = re.compile(r"\b(FOREIGN\s+KEY|UNIQUE)\b",
                             re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL)

# Stored functions have no DuckDB translation. Two shapes, as in the SQLite
# hook: the classic quoted/dollar-quoted `AS` body, which ends at its
# `LANGUAGE <lang>;` line (the body may itself contain `;`), and PostgreSQL
# 14+'s SQL-standard body (`LANGUAGE sql STABLE` ... `RETURN <expr>;`), which
# has no LANGUAGE terminator and ends at its RETURN line. FUNC_BODY_RE gates
# the second rule on the first not applying, so a `RETURN` inside a quoted
# PL/pgSQL body never ends the block early.
FUNC_START_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*CREATE\s+(OR\s+REPLACE\s+)?FUNCTION\b",
                           re.IGNORECASE)
FUNC_END_RE = re.compile(r"\bLANGUAGE\s+\w+\s*;\s*$", re.IGNORECASE)
FUNC_BODY_RE = re.compile(r"\bAS\s*('|\$\w*\$)", re.IGNORECASE)
FUNC_RETURN_END_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*RETURN\b.*;\s*$", re.IGNORECASE)

# Views, and the comments that hang off them, dropped wholesale -- but only
# when a dataset opts in via PGSQL_DROP_VIEWS (see the module docstring).
VIEW_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*CREATE\s+(OR\s+REPLACE\s+)?(MATERIALIZED\s+)?VIEW\b", re.IGNORECASE)
VIEW_NAME_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*CREATE\s+(?:OR\s+REPLACE\s+)?(?:MATERIALIZED\s+)?VIEW\s+([\w.\"]+)",
    re.IGNORECASE)
COMMENT_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*COMMENT\s+ON\b", re.IGNORECASE)
COMMENT_TARGET_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*COMMENT\s+ON\s+(?:MATERIALIZED\s+)?(VIEW|COLUMN)\s+([\w.\"]+)",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# `serial` and friends, as a standalone column type only -- never inside an
# identifier (`serial_no`) or a string.
SERIAL_RE = re.compile(r"\b(big|small)?serial(\d*)\b", re.IGNORECASE)
_SERIAL_TYPE = {"big": "bigint", "small": "smallint", "": "integer"}

# CREATE TABLE is buffered to its terminating ';' so table-level FOREIGN KEY
# constraints can be removed with their preceding comma (dropping the line
# alone would leave a dangling comma before the closing paren).
CREATE_TABLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*CREATE\s+TABLE\b", re.IGNORECASE)
# The referenced column list is optional: PostgreSQL lets `REFERENCES t` stand
# for "t's primary key", which is the spelling northwind's join tables use. The
# referenced table is matched as a single token rather than "anything up to the
# next '('" so that a column-list-less clause cannot swallow the whole of the
# *following* FOREIGN KEY line looking for one -- which left northwind's
# customer_customer_demo as `PRIMARY KEY (...) REFERENCES customers` and a
# parse error.
FK_RE = re.compile(
    r',\s*FOREIGN\s+KEY\s*\([^)]*\)\s*REFERENCES\s+[^\s(,]+(?:\s*\([^)]*\))?'
    r'(?:\s*ON\s+(?:DELETE|UPDATE)\s+'
    r'(?:NO\s+ACTION|CASCADE|RESTRICT|SET\s+NULL|SET\s+DEFAULT))*',
    re.IGNORECASE)

# Schemas whose qualifier is stripped from every emitted line. The default pair
# reproduces the historical behaviour exactly; PGSQL_EXTRA_SCHEMAS extends it
# per-dataset (see the module docstring).
#
# The lookbehind is what keeps the *leading* qualifier of a name the only one
# stripped. A schema may share its name with one of its own tables -- the
# airlines demo's `bookings` schema holds a `bookings` table -- and pg_dump
# writes a column comment on it as the three-part
# `COMMENT ON COLUMN bookings.bookings.book_ref`. A plain `\bbookings\.` takes
# both halves and leaves `COMMENT ON COLUMN book_ref`, a Binder Error with no
# table in it at all; refusing to match after a dot leaves the table behind.
DEFAULT_SCHEMAS = ("public", "cd")
SCHEMA_PREFIX = r"(?i)(?<![\w.])({})\."

# The reader options for the COPY statements PGSQL_COPY_CSV emits. NULLSTR ''
# plus ALLOW_QUOTED_NULLS false is the pair that keeps a NULL (written as a
# bare empty field) distinct from an empty string (written as `""`); with the
# default `allow_quoted_nulls=true` both would arrive as NULL. QUOTE/ESCAPE are
# spelled out rather than left to the defaults so the file format csv_field()
# writes is stated in one place.
CSV_OPTIONS = ("FORMAT CSV, HEADER false, NULLSTR '', "
               "ALLOW_QUOTED_NULLS false, QUOTE '\"', ESCAPE '\"'")

# Characters that force a CSV field to be quoted. A newline or carriage return
# can only reach here from a COPY `\n` / `\r` escape, but quoting them keeps
# the file readable by any RFC4180 parser, DuckDB's included.
CSV_SPECIAL = ',"\n\r'


def type_pattern(name):
    """Compile a column-type spelling from PGSQL_TYPE_MAP into a matcher.

    A trailing ``[]`` becomes a whitespace-tolerant array suffix, so the key
    ``integer[]`` matches ``integer[]`` and ``integer [ ]`` alike; anything
    else is matched as a whole word.
    """
    if name.endswith("[]"):
        return re.compile(r"\b" + re.escape(name[:-2]) + r"\s*\[\s*\]",
                          re.IGNORECASE)
    return re.compile(r"\b" + re.escape(name) + r"\b", re.IGNORECASE)


def configure():
    """(Re)read the environment knobs into module state.

    Called at import and again from main(), so both the exec'd hook and a test
    driving main() see the current environment (the SQLite hook's pattern).
    """
    global EMPTY_BYTEA, SCHEMA_RE, DROP_VIEWS, TYPE_MAP, COPY_CSV
    EMPTY_BYTEA = os.environ.get("PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA", "") == "1"
    extra = tuple(s for s in
                  re.split(r"[\s,]+", os.environ.get("PGSQL_EXTRA_SCHEMAS", ""))
                  if s)
    SCHEMA_RE = re.compile(SCHEMA_PREFIX.format(
        "|".join(map(re.escape, DEFAULT_SCHEMAS + extra))))
    DROP_VIEWS = os.environ.get("PGSQL_DROP_VIEWS", "") == "1"
    TYPE_MAP = [(type_pattern(pair.split("=", 1)[0]), pair.split("=", 1)[1])
                for pair in os.environ.get("PGSQL_TYPE_MAP", "").split()
                if "=" in pair]
    COPY_CSV = os.environ.get("PGSQL_COPY_CSV", "") == "1"


configure()


def detect_encoding(path):
    """Pick a file's encoding -- UTF-8, else Latin-1 -- without loading it.

    world / usda / dellstore ship Latin-1 bytes and DuckDB validates UTF-8, so
    the fallback has to exist; a chunked incremental decode reaches the same
    verdict as decoding the whole file would, in constant memory.
    """
    decoder = codecs.getincrementaldecoder("utf-8")()
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        while True:
            chunk = fh.read(CHUNK)
            try:
                decoder.decode(chunk, not chunk)
            except UnicodeDecodeError:
                return "latin-1"
            if not chunk:
                return "utf-8"


def stream_lines(path, encoding):
    """Yield a file's lines exactly as ``text.split("\\n")`` would.

    That means no trailing newline on each line, and a final empty string when
    the file ends in one -- the convention convert_lines() is written against.
    ``newline="\\n"`` disables universal-newline translation so a lone ``\\r``
    stays inside its line, as splitting on ``\\n`` would leave it.
    """
    with open(path, encoding=encoding, newline="\n") as fh:
        trailing = True
        for raw in fh:
            if raw.endswith("\n"):
                yield raw[:-1]
                trailing = True
            else:
                yield raw
                trailing = False
        if trailing:
            yield ""


def rewrite_empty_bytea(text):
    """`'\\x'` -> `''`, but only when PGSQL_EMPTY_BYTEA=1 (see the constant)."""
    return EMPTY_BYTEA_RE.sub("''", text) if EMPTY_BYTEA else text


def strip_schema(line):
    """Flatten schema qualifiers into DuckDB's default `main` schema."""
    return SCHEMA_RE.sub("", line)


def demote_serial(line):
    """`serial` -> `integer` (DuckDB has no serial pseudo-type)."""
    return SERIAL_RE.sub(
        lambda m: _SERIAL_TYPE[(m.group(1) or "").lower()], line)


def clean_ddl(line):
    """Apply dialect fixes to a single DDL/statement line."""
    line = strip_schema(line)
    line = re.sub(r"(?i)\busing\s+(btree|hash|lsm)\b", "", line)
    line = re.sub(r"(?i)\bwith\s*\(\s*oids[^)]*\)", "", line)
    line = re.sub(r"(?i)::regclass\b", "", line)
    line = demote_serial(line)
    return line


def map_types(statement):
    """Apply the PGSQL_TYPE_MAP substitutions to a CREATE TABLE statement."""
    for pattern, replacement in TYPE_MAP:
        statement = pattern.sub(replacement, statement)
    return statement


def drop_foreign_keys(statement):
    """Remove table-level FOREIGN KEY constraints from a CREATE TABLE block."""
    return FK_RE.sub("", statement)


def view_name(statement):
    """Bare, lower-cased name of the view a CREATE VIEW statement declares."""
    m = VIEW_NAME_RE.match(statement)
    return m.group(1).split(".")[-1].strip('"').lower() if m else None


def comment_target(statement):
    """Bare name of the object a COMMENT ON VIEW / COMMENT ON COLUMN names.

    ``COMMENT ON COLUMN airplanes.model`` targets ``airplanes``, so a comment
    on a dropped view's column can be recognised and dropped with it. Any other
    COMMENT ON shape (TABLE, and the ones a pre-hook has already removed)
    yields None and is therefore never mistaken for a view.
    """
    m = COMMENT_TARGET_RE.match(statement)
    if not m:
        return None
    parts = [p.strip('"').lower() for p in m.group(2).split(".")]
    if m.group(1).upper() == "COLUMN":
        parts = parts[:-1]
    return parts[-1] if parts else None


def split_copy_row(line, delim):
    """Split a COPY TEXT row on unescaped delimiters, keeping escape pairs."""
    fields, cur, i, n = [], [], 0, len(line)
    while i < n:
        c = line[i]
        if c == "\\" and i + 1 < n:
            cur.append(line[i:i + 2]); i += 2; continue
        if c == delim:
            fields.append("".join(cur)); cur = []; i += 1; continue
        cur.append(c); i += 1
    fields.append("".join(cur))
    return fields


_UNESCAPE = {"t": "\t", "n": "\n", "r": "\r", "\\": "\\"}


def unescape(field):
    """Resolve a COPY TEXT field's backslash escapes into real characters."""
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(field):
        if field[i] == "\\" and i + 1 < len(field):
            out.append(_UNESCAPE.get(field[i + 1], field[i + 1])); i += 2
        else:
            out.append(field[i]); i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def copy_value(field):
    """Render one COPY field as a SQL string literal."""
    if field == "\\N":
        return "NULL"
    return "'" + unescape(field).replace("'", "''") + "'"


def csv_field(field):
    """Render one COPY field as an RFC4180 CSV field (PGSQL_COPY_CSV).

    The whole point is that NULL and the empty string stay distinguishable
    after the round trip, which the COPY TEXT format manages with `\\N` and
    CSV cannot do without help: a NULL becomes a bare empty field (which
    ``NULLSTR ''`` reads back as NULL) and an empty string becomes `""` (which
    ``ALLOW_QUOTED_NULLS false`` reads back as ''). Everything else is quoted
    only when it has to be.
    """
    if field == "\\N":
        return ""
    value = unescape(field)
    if value == "" or any(c in value for c in CSV_SPECIAL):
        return '"' + value.replace('"', '""') + '"'
    return value


def keep_alter(statement):
    """True for the one ALTER TABLE form DuckDB implements: ADD PRIMARY KEY."""
    return bool(ALTER_KEEP_RE.search(statement)
                and not ALTER_REJECT_RE.search(statement))


def ends_statement(line, in_string):
    """Track quote state across a line; report whether a ';' closes outside it.

    Multi-row ``INSERT``s (pgexercises ships its data that way) span many lines
    and every one of them is *data*, so they must reach the output untouched --
    running them through clean_ddl would rewrite a value that happens to say
    "serial" or "cd.". Knowing where the statement ends needs quote state, not
    a bare ``';' in line``: a literal may itself contain a semicolon, and a
    doubled ``''`` inside one is an escaped quote, not a close.
    """
    i, n, done = 0, len(line), False
    while i < n:
        c = line[i]
        if in_string:
            if c == "'" and i + 1 < n and line[i + 1] == "'":
                i += 2; continue
            if c == "'":
                in_string = False
        elif c == "'":
            in_string = True
        elif c == ";":
            done = True
        i += 1
    return in_string, done


def read_statement(src, first):
    """Collect lines from ``first`` up to the ';' that closes the statement.

    Returns ``(statement, next_line)`` -- the joined statement and the first
    line past it (None at end of input), so the caller never has to look ahead
    itself. Unterminated input yields the remainder rather than looping.
    """
    block = [first]
    in_string, done = ends_statement(first, False)
    while not done:
        line = next(src, None)
        if line is None:
            break
        block.append(line)
        in_string, done = ends_statement(line, in_string)
    return "\n".join(block), next(src, None)


def convert_copy(match, src, out, csv_dir):
    """Convert one ``COPY ... FROM stdin`` block; return the next line.

    Either batched multi-row ``INSERT``s (the default) or, under
    PGSQL_COPY_CSV, a sibling ``<table>.csv`` plus the one ``COPY ... FROM
    '<file>'`` that loads it.
    """
    table, cols, delim = match.group(1), match.group(2), match.group(3) or "\t"
    table = table.split(".")[-1]

    if COPY_CSV:
        path = os.path.join(csv_dir, table + ".csv")
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as csv_out:
            line = next(src, None)
            while line is not None and line.rstrip() != "\\.":
                csv_out.write(",".join(csv_field(f) for f in
                                       split_copy_row(line, delim)) + "\n")
                line = next(src, None)
        out.write("COPY {} ({}) FROM '{}' ({});\n".format(
            table, cols, path.replace("'", "''"), CSV_OPTIONS))
        return next(src, None)

    head = "INSERT INTO {} ({}) VALUES\n".format(table, cols)
    batch = []
    line = next(src, None)
    while line is not None and line.rstrip() != "\\.":
        row = split_copy_row(line, delim)
        batch.append("(" + ", ".join(copy_value(f) for f in row) + ")")
        if len(batch) >= BATCH:
            out.write(head + ",\n".join(batch) + ";\n")
            batch = []
        line = next(src, None)
    if batch:
        out.write(head + ",\n".join(batch) + ";\n")
    return next(src, None)  # skip the \. terminator


def convert_lines(lines, out, csv_dir=""):
    """Rewrite a dump, line by line, into ``out``.

    ``lines`` is any iterable of newline-free lines (main() streams them off
    disk; the tests pass a split string). ``csv_dir`` is where PGSQL_COPY_CSV
    writes its sidecar files -- the directory holding the SQL file, so the
    relative path the emitted COPY carries resolves from the build dir the
    loader stage runs in (empty, the usual case, means alongside it).
    """
    src = iter(lines)
    dropped_views = set()
    drop_func = func_has_body = False
    line = next(src, None)
    while line is not None:
        if drop_func:
            if FUNC_BODY_RE.search(line):
                func_has_body = True
            if FUNC_END_RE.search(line) or \
                    (not func_has_body and FUNC_RETURN_END_RE.match(line)):
                drop_func = False
            line = next(src, None)
            continue

        m = COPY_RE.match(line)
        if m:
            line = convert_copy(m, src, out, csv_dir)
            continue

        if FUNC_START_RE.match(line):
            func_has_body = bool(FUNC_BODY_RE.search(line))
            drop_func = not FUNC_END_RE.search(line)
            line = next(src, None)
            continue

        if CREATE_TABLE_RE.match(line):
            # Buffered whole so a table-level FOREIGN KEY can be removed along
            # with the comma that precedes it -- a per-line drop would leave a
            # dangling comma before the closing paren -- and so PGSQL_TYPE_MAP
            # applies to column declarations and nothing else.
            statement, line = read_statement(src, line)
            out.write(map_types(drop_foreign_keys(clean_ddl(statement))) + "\n")
            continue

        if ALTER_TABLE_RE.match(line):
            # Buffered too (pg_dump wraps ALTER TABLE over two lines), because
            # the verb that decides whether DuckDB can run it -- PRIMARY KEY vs
            # FOREIGN KEY / UNIQUE / EXCLUDE / IDENTITY / OWNER TO -- is on the
            # continuation line.
            statement, line = read_statement(src, line)
            if keep_alter(statement):
                out.write(clean_ddl(statement) + "\n")
            continue

        if DROP_VIEWS and VIEW_RE.match(line):
            statement, line = read_statement(src, line)
            name = view_name(strip_schema(statement))
            if name:
                dropped_views.add(name)
            continue

        if dropped_views and COMMENT_RE.match(line):
            # Only reachable under PGSQL_DROP_VIEWS, and only once a view has
            # actually been dropped: a comment on one is a Catalog Error, while
            # every COMMENT ON TABLE/COLUMN of a surviving table is kept.
            statement, line = read_statement(src, line)
            if comment_target(strip_schema(statement)) not in dropped_views:
                out.write(clean_ddl(statement) + "\n")
            continue

        if DROP_BLOCK_RE.match(line):
            # Buffered and dropped whole: a CREATE DOMAIN may wrap onto a
            # second line (a CHECK or a DEFAULT), and half of one left behind
            # is a parse error of its own.
            _, line = read_statement(src, line)
            continue

        if DROP_LINE_RE.match(line):
            line = next(src, None)
            continue

        stripped = line.lstrip()
        if stripped.startswith("--") or not stripped:
            out.write(line + "\n")
        elif stripped.upper().startswith("INSERT INTO"):
            # Only the header names the table (and so may carry a schema
            # qualifier); every following line is data, copied verbatim.
            out.write(strip_schema(line) + "\n")
            in_string, done = ends_statement(line, False)
            while not done:
                line = next(src, None)
                if line is None:
                    break
                out.write(line + "\n")
                in_string, done = ends_statement(line, in_string)
            line = next(src, None)
            continue
        else:
            out.write(clean_ddl(line) + "\n")
        line = next(src, None)


def convert_file(text, out, csv_dir=""):
    """convert_lines() over a whole dump held in memory (the tests' entry)."""
    convert_lines(iter(text.split("\n")), out, csv_dir)


def main():
    configure()
    files = os.environ.get("SQL_FILES", "").split() or sorted(glob.glob("*.sql"))
    files = [f for f in files if os.path.isfile(f)]
    if not files:
        sys.exit("pgsql transform: no SQL files to convert")

    for path in files:
        # Rewritten through a sibling temporary rather than in place: the file
        # is streamed, so its old bytes are still being read while the new ones
        # are written. os.replace makes the swap atomic.
        encoding = detect_encoding(path)
        tmp = path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as out:
            convert_lines((rewrite_empty_bytea(line)
                           for line in stream_lines(path, encoding)),
                          out, os.path.dirname(path))
        os.replace(tmp, path)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
